---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Resources and Development | CBSE Class 10 Geography | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Geography Chapter 1 study notes on Resources and Development. Classification of resources, sustainable development, Agenda 21, resource planning in India, land use pattern, land degradation and conservation, soil types (alluvial, black, red/yellow, laterite, arid, forest) and soil erosion with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Resources and Development
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Geography Chapter 1</span>
      <h1>Resources and Development</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Study the foundation of geography: resource classification, sustainable resource planning, land utilization dynamics, soil profiles across India, and erosion mitigation measures.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Core Resource Classification &amp; Soil Profiles</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌍 Classification of Resources &amp; Planning</h3>
          <p class="formula">Origin, Exhaustibility, Ownership &amp; Status of Development</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>On the basis of Origin:</strong> Biotic (living: flora, fauna, human beings) and Abiotic (non-living: rocks, metals).</li>
            <li><strong>Exhaustibility:</strong> Renewable (replenished: solar, wind, forests) and Non-Renewable (finite: coal, petroleum, taking millions of years to form).</li>
            <li><strong>Ownership:</strong> Individual, Community (grazing grounds, burial spots), National (all minerals and territorial waters up to 12 nautical miles / 22.2 km from coastline), and International (Exclusive Economic Zone beyond 200 nautical miles governed by international bodies).</li>
            <li><strong>Status of Development:</strong> Potential, Developed, Stock (hydrogen in water lack technology to extract), and Reserves (subset of stock tapped with current technology, like river water for hydro-power).</li>
            <li><strong>Resource Planning in India:</strong> 3-stage process: (i) Identification and inventory through survey and mapping; (ii) Planning structure with appropriate technology, skill, and institutional set-up; (iii) Matching resource plans with overall national development plans.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🗺️ Land Resources &amp; Degradation</h3>
          <p class="formula">Relief: 43% Plains, 30% Mountains, 27% Plateaus</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>India's Land Relief:</strong> 43% plains (agriculture &amp; industry), 30% mountains (perennial rivers, tourism), 27% plateaus (rich in minerals, fossil fuels, and forests).</li>
            <li><strong>Land Use Pattern:</strong> Net Sown Area is over 80% in Punjab &amp; Haryana, but less than 10% in Arunachal Pradesh, Mizoram, and Andaman &amp; Nicobar. Forest cover in India is below the desired 33% outlined in the National Forest Policy (1952).</li>
            <li><strong>Causes of Land Degradation:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Deforestation &amp; Mining:</em> Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, MP, Odisha (abandoned open-cast mining sites).
              <br/>• <em>Overgrazing:</em> Gujarat, Rajasthan, MP, Maharashtra.
              <br/>• <em>Over-irrigation:</em> Punjab, Haryana, Western UP (waterlogging leading to soil salinity and alkalinity).
              <br/>• <em>Industrial effluents:</em> Untreated chemical effluents polluting soil and groundwater.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌱 Soil Classification: 6 Major Soil Types</h3>
          <p class="formula">Most Important Renewable Natural Resource</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Alluvial Soil:</strong> Widely spread in Northern plains and coastal deltas (Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Kaveri). Deposited by Indus, Ganga, Brahmaputra. Rich in potash, phosphoric acid, and lime; ideal for sugarcane, paddy, wheat. Divided into <strong>Khadar</strong> (new, fine, fertile) and <strong>Bhangar</strong> (old, high kankar nodules).</li>
            <li><strong>2. Black Soil (Regur / Black Cotton):</strong> Formed from Deccan basalt lava traps (Maharashtra, Saurashtra, Malwa, MP). Rich in calcium carbonate, magnesium, potash, lime; highly moisture-retentive; develops deep cracks during hot weather.</li>
            <li><strong>3. Red and Yellow Soil:</strong> Develops on crystalline igneous rocks in areas of low rainfall (Eastern &amp; Southern Deccan, Odisha, Chhattisgarh). Reddish due to iron diffusion; yellow in hydrated form.</li>
            <li><strong>4. Laterite Soil:</strong> Formed under high temperature and heavy rainfall (leaching). Humus poor. Found in Karnataka, Kerala, Tamil Nadu, MP, Assam. Ideal for tea, coffee, and cashew nuts (with fertilizers).</li>
            <li><strong>5. Arid Soil:</strong> Sandy, saline, dry, humus-lacking (Western Rajasthan). Cultivable only with irrigation (e.g. Indira Gandhi Canal).</li>
            <li><strong>6. Forest/Mountain Soil:</strong> Loamy and silty in valley sides and coarse-grained in upper slopes; acidic with low humus in snow-covered areas.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🛡️ Soil Erosion &amp; Conservation</h3>
          <p class="formula">Denudation and Washing Away of Soil Cover</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Types of Water Erosion:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Gully Erosion:</em> Running water cuts through clayey soils making deep channels called gullies. Renders land unfit for cultivation, creating <strong>badlands / ravines</strong> (e.g. Chambal basin).
              <br/>• <em>Sheet Erosion:</em> Water flows as a broad sheet over large areas down a slope, washing away the entire topsoil.
              <br/>• <em>Wind Erosion:</em> Wind blows loose, dry soil off flat or sloping land.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Soil Conservation Methods:</strong>
              <br/>• <strong>Contour Ploughing:</strong> Ploughing along contour lines decelerates water flow down slopes.
              <br/>• <strong>Terrace Farming:</strong> Steps cut into slopes restrict erosion (Western and Central Himalayas).
              <br/>• <strong>Strip Cropping:</strong> Strips of grass left between crops break the force of wind.
              <br/>• <strong>Shelter Belts:</strong> Planting lines of trees creates windbreaks, stabilizing sand dunes in coastal/desert regions.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Soil Texture/Profile &amp; Conservation Architecture</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · Layers of Soil Profile</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Layers of Soil Profile">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Layer 1: Topsoil -->
              <rect x="40" y="30" width="380" height="45" fill="#15803d" rx="4"/>
              <text x="230" y="52" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Topsoil (Upper Layer): Humus &amp; Living Organisms</text>
              <text x="230" y="66" fill="#bbf7d0" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Rich in organic matter, supports all plant root systems</text>

              <!-- Layer 2: Subsoil -->
              <rect x="40" y="80" width="380" height="50" fill="#b45309" rx="4"/>
              <text x="230" y="104" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Subsoil: Weathered Rocks, Sand, Silt &amp; Clay</text>
              <text x="230" y="120" fill="#fde68a" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Accumulation of minerals and iron compounds, lower humus</text>

              <!-- Layer 3: Substratum -->
              <rect x="40" y="135" width="380" height="45" fill="#64748b" rx="4"/>
              <text x="230" y="157" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Substratum: Weathered Parent Rock Material</text>
              <text x="230" y="171" fill="#e2e8f0" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Partially broken rock fragments</text>

              <!-- Layer 4: Bedrock -->
              <rect x="40" y="185" width="380" height="40" fill="#334155" rx="4"/>
              <text x="230" y="209" fill="#ffffff" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Unweathered Parent Bedrock</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Standard pedological profile showing the topsoil humus layer down to the underlying parent rock foundation.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · Soil Conservation Techniques</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Soil conservation methods">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Technique 1: Contour Ploughing -->
              <rect x="25" y="40" width="195" height="90" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="122" y="65" fill="#1d4ed8" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Contour Ploughing</text>
              <text x="122" y="85" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Ploughing along altitude contours</text>
              <text x="122" y="105" fill="#166534" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Slows down downhill water runoff</text>

              <!-- Technique 2: Terrace Farming -->
              <rect x="240" y="40" width="195" height="90" fill="#f0fdf4" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="337" y="65" fill="#15803d" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Terrace Farming</text>
              <text x="337" y="85" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Cutting steps into steep hill slopes</text>
              <text x="337" y="105" fill="#166534" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Prevents soil wash in Himalayas</text>

              <!-- Technique 3: Strip Cropping -->
              <rect x="25" y="145" width="195" height="85" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="122" y="170" fill="#b45309" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Strip Cropping</text>
              <text x="122" y="190" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Strips of grass grown between crops</text>
              <text x="122" y="210" fill="#166534" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Breaks wind velocity</text>

              <!-- Technique 4: Shelter Belts -->
              <rect x="240" y="145" width="195" height="85" fill="#fdf4ff" stroke="#a855f7" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="337" y="170" fill="#7e22ce" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Shelter Belts</text>
              <text x="337" y="190" fill="#334155" font-size="9" text-anchor="middle">Rows of trees planted along borders</text>
              <text x="337" y="210" fill="#166534" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Stabilizes coastal &amp; desert dunes</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">Four vital agricultural engineering practices that arrest soil erosion caused by wind, gravity, and water runoff.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Board Exam Quiz Section (20 Questions) -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Quiz</h2>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0;">Test your understanding of Resource Classification, Soils of India, Land Degradation, and Erosion.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizContainer">
        <!-- Rendered via script -->
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <button type="button" class="button" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
        <span class="small" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action Banner -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Excel in CBSE Class 10 Geography</h2>
      <p>Master soil maps, land utilization tables, resource planning models, and board answer presentation techniques.</p>
      <a href={calBookingUrl} class="button">Book a Doubt Clearing Session</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "1. What is the territorial water limit up to which oceanic resources belong to the nation?",
      options: [
        "12 nautical miles (approx 22.2 km) from the coast",
        "200 nautical miles from the baseline",
        "1 nautical mile only",
        "50 kilometers from port cities"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Oceanic area up to 12 nautical miles (22.2 km) from the coast is termed territorial water and resources therein belong to the nation."
    },
    {
      q: "2. Oceanic resources beyond 200 nautical miles of the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ) belong to which category?",
      options: [
        "Individual Resources",
        "National Resources",
        "International Resources governed by international institutions",
        "Community Resources"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      explanation: "Open ocean resources beyond 200 nautical miles of the EEZ belong to international institutions, and no individual country can utilize them without consent."
    },
    {
      q: "3. What was the historic declaration signed at the United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (UNCED) in Rio de Janeiro in 1992?",
      options: [
        "Kyoto Protocol",
        "Agenda 21",
        "Paris Climate Agreement",
        "Montreal Protocol"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Agenda 21 was adopted at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit to achieve global sustainable development and combat environmental damage, poverty, and disease."
    },
    {
      q: "4. What desired percentage of geographical area should be under forest cover according to the National Forest Policy (1952)?",
      options: [
        "20%",
        "25%",
        "33%",
        "50%"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      explanation: "The National Forest Policy (1952) outlined that 33% of the geographical area should be under forest cover to maintain ecological equilibrium."
    },
    {
      q: "5. In which Indian states is deforestation due to mining the primary cause of severe land degradation?",
      options: [
        "Punjab and Haryana",
        "Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh, and Odisha",
        "Gujarat and Rajasthan",
        "Kerala and Tamil Nadu"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Open-cast mining and left-behind deep scars in Jharkhand, Chhattisgarh, MP, and Odisha have caused extensive forest loss and land degradation."
    },
    {
      q: "6. What is the major cause of land degradation in Punjab, Haryana, and Western Uttar Pradesh?",
      options: [
        "Overgrazing",
        "Over-irrigation leading to waterlogging, increased salinity, and alkalinity in the soil",
        "Industrial air pollution",
        "Forest fires"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Intensive canal irrigation in north-western green revolution belts causes waterlogging and soil salinization, degrading soil fertility."
    },
    {
      q: "7. Which soil is the most widely spread and important soil of India, covering the northern plains and river deltas?",
      options: [
        "Black Soil",
        "Alluvial Soil",
        "Laterite Soil",
        "Arid Soil"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Alluvial soil is the most widespread soil in India, deposited by the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra river systems."
    },
    {
      q: "8. What is the difference between 'Khadar' and 'Bhangar' alluvial soil?",
      options: [
        "Khadar is old alluvium with kankar; Bhangar is new alluvium",
        "Khadar is new, fine, fertile alluvium; Bhangar is older alluvium containing higher concentration of kankar nodules",
        "Khadar is found only on mountain peaks; Bhangar is found in deserts",
        "Khadar is black in color; Bhangar is yellow"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Khadar is the younger, frequently flooded, more fertile alluvium, whereas Bhangar is the older terrace alluvium containing calcareous kankar nodules."
    },
    {
      q: "9. Which soil type is also famously known as 'Regur Soil' and is ideal for growing cotton?",
      options: [
        "Laterite Soil",
        "Black Soil",
        "Red Soil",
        "Forest Soil"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Black soil is called Regur soil or black cotton soil, formed by basaltic lava flows over the Deccan trap."
    },
    {
      q: "10. What unique physical property does Black Soil exhibit during the dry summer season?",
      options: [
        "It dissolves into liquid mud",
        "It develops deep cracks, which aids in proper aeration of the soil",
        "It turns into pure sand",
        "It expands by 200% in volume"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Because of its high clay content and moisture retention, black soil develops wide cracks during hot dry weather, facilitating natural aeration."
    },
    {
      q: "11. Why does Red Soil look reddish in color?",
      options: [
        "Due to diffusion of iron in crystalline and metamorphic rocks",
        "Due to chemical pollution from copper factories",
        "Because it is mixed with brick dust",
        "Due to high volcanic ash deposition"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Red soils develop a reddish color due to a wide diffusion of iron compounds in crystalline igneous and metamorphic rocks (appearing yellow when hydrated)."
    },
    {
      q: "12. In which regions does 'Laterite Soil' typically develop?",
      options: [
        "In cold alpine deserts with sub-zero temperatures",
        "In areas with high temperatures and heavy tropical rainfall, resulting in intense leaching",
        "In dry alluvial plains of western Rajasthan",
        "Along glacier margins in the Himalayas"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Laterite soil forms under conditions of high temperature and intense monsoon rainfall, which causes heavy leaching of silica and lime."
    },
    {
      q: "13. Laterite soil with adequate doses of manure and fertilizers is particularly suitable for which crops in Karnataka, Kerala, and Tamil Nadu?",
      options: [
        "Wheat and barley",
        "Tea, coffee, and cashew nuts",
        "Jute and cotton",
        "Mustard and gram"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Laterite soil in the Western Ghats and southern states is ideal for plantation crops like tea, coffee, and cashew nut."
    },
    {
      q: "14. What is 'Gully Erosion'?",
      options: [
        "When running water carves out deep channels through clayey soil, creating ravines or badlands",
        "When wind blows sand over agricultural fields",
        "When sea waves wash away sandy beaches",
        "When glacier ice melts into mountain rivers"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Gully erosion occurs when storm runoff cuts deep gullies into the landscape, rendering the land uncultivable (badland), as seen in the Chambal ravines."
    },
    {
      q: "15. What is 'Sheet Erosion'?",
      options: [
        "When topsoil is washed away as a broad sheet over large areas down a gentle slope",
        "When farmers use plastic sheets over crops",
        "When ice sheets slide down mountain glaciers",
        "When bedrock is cut by miners"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Sheet erosion is the uniform removal of a thin layer of topsoil over large tracts of land by the broad sheet flow of rainwater."
    },
    {
      q: "16. How does 'Contour Ploughing' prevent soil erosion?",
      options: [
        "By digging underground tunnels for water",
        "By ploughing along the elevation contours of a hill, which decelerates the downhill flow of runoff water",
        "By removing all rocks from the hillsides",
        "By covering the hillside in concrete"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Ploughing parallel to contour lines creates natural ridges that interrupt downhill water flow, drastically reducing erosion on slopes."
    },
    {
      q: "17. In which region of India is 'Terrace Farming' extensively practiced to conserve soil on mountain slopes?",
      options: [
        "Thar Desert of Rajasthan",
        "Western and Central Himalayas",
        "Coastal plains of Odisha",
        "Sundarbans Delta"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Terrace cultivation is practiced on the rugged slopes of the Western and Central Himalayas (Himachal, Uttarakhand) to prevent soil runoff."
    },
    {
      q: "18. What are 'Shelter Belts' and why are they planted?",
      options: [
        "Rows of trees planted to break the force of wind and stabilize coastal sand dunes and desert boundaries",
        "Emergency concrete shelters built against cyclones",
        "Fences made of metal wires to catch rainwater",
        "Underground storage bunkers for grain"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Shelter belts consist of linear tree plantings that act as windbreaks, preventing wind erosion and stabilizing sand dunes in arid and coastal zones."
    },
    {
      q: "19. What are 'Stock' resources?",
      options: [
        "Materials in the environment that have the potential to satisfy human needs, but human beings do not possess appropriate technology to access them",
        "Food items stocked in government warehouses",
        "Shares bought on the national stock exchange",
        "Water stored in municipal water tanks"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Stock refers to natural substances (e.g. hydrogen and oxygen in water as clean fuel sources) that cannot be tapped currently due to technological limitations."
    },
    {
      q: "20. What is 'Strip Cropping'?",
      options: [
        "Growing wide strips of grass between cultivated crops to break the velocity of wind",
        "Harvesting crops with motorized combine harvesters",
        "Stripping all trees from riverbanks",
        "Growing only one single crop species across an entire state"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Strip cropping involves dividing large open fields into strips where bands of grass are grown alongside crops to cushion and diminish the force of winds."
    }
  ];

  function renderQuiz() {
    const container = document.getElementById('quizContainer');
    if (!container) return;
    container.innerHTML = '';

    questions.forEach((item, idx) => {
      const qCard = document.createElement('div');
      qCard.className = 'quiz-card';
      qCard.id = `qCard_${idx}`;

      const title = document.createElement('h3');
      title.className = 'quiz-question';
      title.textContent = item.q;
      qCard.appendChild(title);

      const optList = document.createElement('div');
      optList.className = 'quiz-options';

      item.options.forEach((optText, optIdx) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'quiz-opt-btn';
        btn.textContent = optText;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => handleSelectOption(idx, optIdx));
        optList.appendChild(btn);
      });
      qCard.appendChild(optList);

      const feedback = document.createElement('div');
      feedback.className = 'quiz-feedback';
      feedback.id = `feedback_${idx}`;
      qCard.appendChild(feedback);

      container.appendChild(qCard);
    });
  }

  let userAnswers = {};

  function handleSelectOption(qIdx, optIdx) {
    if (userAnswers[qIdx] !== undefined) return;
    userAnswers[qIdx] = optIdx;

    const q = questions[qIdx];
    const isCorrect = (optIdx === q.correct);
    const card = document.getElementById(`qCard_${qIdx}`);
    const btns = card.querySelectorAll('.quiz-opt-btn');
    const fb = document.getElementById(`feedback_${qIdx}`);

    btns.forEach((btn, i) => {
      btn.disabled = true;
      if (i === q.correct) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-correct');
      } else if (i === optIdx) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-wrong');
      }
    });

    if (isCorrect) {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-correct';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Correct!</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    } else {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-wrong';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Incorrect.</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    }

    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  function updateScoreSummary() {
    let score = 0;
    const answeredCount = Object.keys(userAnswers).length;
    for (let qIdx in userAnswers) {
      if (userAnswers[qIdx] === questions[qIdx].correct) {
        score++;
      }
    }
    const badge = document.getElementById('quizScoreBadge');
    if (badge) badge.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${questions.length}`;

    const prog = document.getElementById('quizProgressText');
    if (prog) prog.textContent = `${answeredCount} of ${questions.length} answered`;
  }

  function resetQuiz() {
    userAnswers = {};
    renderQuiz();
    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    renderQuiz();
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('resetQuizBtn');
    if (resetBtn) resetBtn.addEventListener('click', resetQuiz);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem;
  }

  nav.small {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  nav.small a {
    color: var(--brand);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .resource-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--brand);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .section {
    margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
  }

  .section h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
